it's winter in southern chile home to  the alerse or patagonian cypress one of  the world's two oldest proven tree  species  that's where we meet up with  environmental scientist jonathan  barichivic from the university of paris  in the alessi national park a unique  temperate rainforest  he actually grew up here when his  grandfather was a park ranger  he was the one who discovered a new  challenger to the world's oldest  recorded non-clonal tree  a four thousand eight hundred fifty  three-year-old bristlecone pine in  california  but ichivich is taking us to meet  great-grandfather the nickname of the  alerse he estimates is almost  5500 years old  but first he demonstrates the  traditional method for determining the  age of trees by using a special drill or  increment core to remove a five  millimeter sample the length of the  tree's radius here is you got a a core  where you can see three rings as like a  uh barcode this then you transform it to  two numbers then with this number we  build models of how the tree grows  unlike satellite data that goes back no  more than 40 years trees reveal earth's  environment as far back as prehistoric  times  but ichivich argues that the past can be  used to predict future climate change  more accurately the core of a tree is  something like a book it tells the story  not just of the tree but of the whole  area in the last decades hundreds of  years or thousands of years  to read it you can look at the rings  these for example show that it was a  plentiful year they're very separated so  it means there was a lot of rain the  ones that are much closer indicates that  those years were of drought and over  here you see the scar of the tree this  means that there was a fire  but dentro chronologist antonio lara who  recorded a three thousand six hundred  twenty year old alersif thirty years ago  says in  tourism is endangering the survival of  these trees whose roots are already  dangerously exposed from erosion this is  a younger alersi tree and it was someone  that we don't know who a couple of weeks  ago came and you can see here like with  a machete or something like that took  out the the bark we don't know why  because this is pure vandalism  as a result the park is now temporarily  closed to the public but the forestry  service made an exception to allow us to  see great grandfather for ourselves  perched deep in an isolated ravine it's  an imposing majestic sight to behold  from its four meter wide trunk  to its enormous exposed roots  the longest instrument available isn't  long enough to reach the tree's center  to determine its age with the  traditional method so barichivitch has  devised a mathematical model to fill in  the gaps but some dentro chronologists  aren't yet convinced i think that it's a  bit hard without having actual ring  counts of the trees it's we cannot  really say that it's  definitely the oldest we can  of course say that it is amongst the  oldest trees in the world  but ichivich will be publishing a paper  later this year defending his theory the  great grandfather is indeed 540  years old but i'm not worried because  this is science for the planet  it's science that make us to act on time  to protect  what  is left of a planet that was once  completely wild  he insists the challenge is to use  ancient trees to improve climate models  to determine how forests might adapt to  our changing conditions  the same forests on which the future of  humanity also depends  lucia newman al jazeera los lagos region  chile
 

